# content/timelog.json is GENERATED, never hand-edited: scripts/sync-timelog.mjs
# rewrites it from the local timewarrior database and .githooks/pre-commit stages
# it on every commit where the figures moved.
#
# That makes it a conflict magnet. Two branches that each commit while a session
# is running both touch the same day's `seconds`, so an ordinary `git rebase` or
# `git merge` stops on a textual conflict in a file nobody typed — and because
# the hook deliberately goes inert mid-replay, you are left resolving it by hand.
#
# `merge=ours` resolves it the only way that makes sense for a derived file:
# keep one side, and let the next commit regenerate it from the real source of
# truth, which is the database rather than either branch. Nothing is lost —
# whatever the merge keeps is replaced by live data on the next sync.
#
# The driver is a built-in no-op that still has to be declared:
#   git config merge.ours.driver true
# scripts/install-hooks.mjs sets that alongside core.hooksPath, so a fresh
# clone gets it from `npm install` without anyone having to know this file
# exists. Without the driver declared, git silently falls back to a normal
# merge and the conflicts come back.
content/timelog.json merge=ours
